horLink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</a></h3>
<p> Pearson correlation analysis was conducted to investigate the relations=
hips between concrete mix variables and compressive strength.</p>
<p> The results showed that total binder had the strongest positive correla=
tion with compressive strength (0.598), followed by log age (0.560) and cem=
ent (0.488).</p>
<p> Water-to-cement ratio showed a moderate negative correlation (-0.489), =
indicating that higher water-to-cement ratios tend to be associated with lo=
wer concrete strength.</p>
<p> Water quantity also showed a negative correlation (-0.270).</p>
<p> Among the original individual ingredients, cement demonstrated the stro=
ngest positive correlation with compressive strength.</p>
<p> The logarithmic transformation of curing age produced a stronger correl=
ation (0.560) than the original age variable (0.337), supporting the use of=
 feature engineering to represent nonlinear curing behaviour.</p>
<p> <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Total binder, curing age and water-to-ceme=
nt ratio appear to be important variables for understanding concrete streng=
th. These relationships will be investigated further during model developme=
nt.</p>
