mpressive-Strength"><span class=3D"numbering-entry"></span>EDA 3: Relations=
hip Between Curing Age and Concrete Compressive Strength<a target=3D"_self"=
 href=3D"http://localhost:8888/notebooks/Downloads/MLCapstone.ipynb#EDA-3:-=
Relationship-Between-Curing-Age-and-Concrete-Compressive-Strength" class=3D=
"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</a></h3>
<p> Scatterplot analysis and LOWESS smoothing were used to investigate the =
relationship between curing age and concrete compressive strength.</p>
<p> The results indicate that concrete strength generally increases rapidly=
 during the early curing period. However, the rate of strength development =
becomes slower as curing age increases.</p>
<p> The LOWESS trend line demonstrates a nonlinear relationship rather than=
 a straight-line pattern.</p>
<p> There is also considerable variation in strength measurements at the sa=
me curing age, indicating that other factors, including ingredient quantiti=
es and mix proportions, influence compressive strength.</p>
<p> The trend at older curing ages should be interpreted cautiously because=
 fewer observations are available in those age ranges.</p>
<p> <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Curing age has a nonlinear relationship wi=
th concrete compressive strength. This supports the use of the logarithmic =
age feature and the investigation of nonlinear machine learning models such=
 as Random Forest.</p>
