-line"><span class=3D"=CD=BCr =CD=BCp">####</span><span class=3D"=CD=BCr"> =
The 10 largest prediction errors from the Random Forest model were investig=
ated to understand where the model struggled.</span></div><div class=3D"cm-=
line"><br></div><div class=3D"cm-line"> The model showed a tendency to unde=
restimate some high-strength concrete samples, particularly mixes with high=
 total binder content. For example, one mix with a total binder content of =
606.5 kg/m=C2=B3 and a curing age of 56 days had an actual compressive stre=
ngth of 80.20 MPa, compared with a predicted strength of 65.29 MPa.</div><d=
iv class=3D"cm-line"><br></div><div class=3D"cm-line"> The model also overe=
stimated the strength of some lower-strength mixes. One observation with a =
water-cement ratio of 1.02 had an actual strength of 23.25 MPa, while the m=
odel predicted 35.74 MPa.</div><div class=3D"cm-line"><br></div><div class=
=3D"cm-line"> Some of the largest errors also occurred in samples with shor=
t curing periods of 3=E2=80=937 days, suggesting that early-age strength pr=
ediction may be challenging.</div><div class=3D"cm-line"><br></div><div cla=
ss=3D"cm-line"> Overall, the investigation indicates that although Random F=
orest achieved strong overall performance, it was less accurate for certain=
 unusual mix compositions and strength levels. These findings highlight the=
 importance of examining individual prediction errors rather than relying s=
olely on aggregate performance metrics.</div></div><div class=3D"cm-layer c=
