=3D"text/markdown"><h4 data-jupyter-id=3D"The-10-largest-prediction-errors-=
from-the-Random-Forest-model-were-investigated-to-understand-where-the-mode=
l-struggled."><span class=3D"numbering-entry"></span>The 10 largest predict=
ion errors from the Random Forest model were investigated to understand whe=
re the model struggled.<a target=3D"_self" href=3D"http://localhost:8888/no=
tebooks/Downloads/MLCapstone.ipynb#The-10-largest-prediction-errors-from-th=
e-Random-Forest-model-were-investigated-to-understand-where-the-model-strug=
gled." class=3D"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</a></h4>
<p> The model showed a tendency to underestimate some high-strength concret=
e samples, particularly mixes with high total binder content. For example, =
one mix with a total binder content of 606.5 kg/m=C2=B3 and a curing age of=
 56 days had an actual compressive strength of 80.20 MPa, compared with a p=
redicted strength of 65.29 MPa.</p>
<p> The model also overestimated the strength of some lower-strength mixes.=
 One observation with a water-cement ratio of 1.02 had an actual strength o=
f 23.25 MPa, while the model predicted 35.74 MPa.</p>
<p> Some of the largest errors also occurred in samples with short curing p=
eriods of 3=E2=80=937 days, suggesting that early-age strength prediction m=
ay be challenging.</p>
<p> Overall, the investigation indicates that although Random Forest achiev=
ed strong overall performance, it was less accurate for certain unusual mix=
 compositions and strength levels. These findings highlight the importance =
