 correlation with compressive strength among the ingredients, at approximat=
ely <span class=3D"=CD=BC1a =CD=BCp">**</span><span class=3D"=CD=BC1a">0.48=
8</span><span class=3D"=CD=BC1a =CD=BCp">**</span>. This suggests that high=
er cement content is generally associated with higher concrete strength, al=
though strength depends on the combined mix composition.</div><div class=3D=
"cm-line"><br></div><div class=3D"cm-line"><span class=3D"=CD=BCr =CD=BCp">=
###</span><span class=3D"=CD=BCr"> 3. Effect of Curing Age</span></div><div=
 class=3D"cm-line"> Concrete strength generally increased as curing age inc=
reased. However, the relationship was nonlinear: strength increased more ra=
pidly during earlier curing periods and tended to grow more gradually at la=
ter ages. This indicates that a simple straight-line relationship does not =
fully explain strength development.</div><div class=3D"cm-line"><br></div><=
