</div><div class=3D"lm-Widget jp-RenderedHTMLCommon jp-RenderedMarkdown jp-=
MarkdownOutput" data-mime-type=3D"text/markdown"><h4 data-jupyter-id=3D"The=
-analysis-of-the-Concrete-Compressive-Strength-dataset-revealed-several-imp=
ortant-relationships-between-concrete-mix-composition,-curing-age-and-compr=
essive-strength." style=3D"">The analysis of the Concrete Compressive Stren=
gth dataset revealed several important relationships between concrete mix c=
omposition, curing age and compressive strength.<a target=3D"_self" href=3D=
"http://localhost:8888/notebooks/Downloads/MLCapstone.ipynb#The-analysis-of=
-the-Concrete-Compressive-Strength-dataset-revealed-several-important-relat=
ionships-between-concrete-mix-composition,-curing-age-and-compressive-stren=
gth." class=3D"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</a></h4>
<h3 data-jupyter-id=3D"1.-Distribution-of-Concrete-Strength">1. Distributio=
n of Concrete Strength<a target=3D"_self" href=3D"http://localhost:8888/not=
ebooks/Downloads/MLCapstone.ipynb#1.-Distribution-of-Concrete-Strength" cla=
ss=3D"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</a></h3>
<p> The compressive strength values were slightly right-skewed, with a mean=
 of <strong>35.82 MPa</strong> and a median of <strong>34.45 MPa</strong>. =
This indicates that a smaller number of high-strength concrete samples pull=
 the average above the median. Strength values ranged from approximately 2.=
33 MPa to 82.60 MPa.</p>
<h3 data-jupyter-id=3D"2.-Most-Influential-Individual-Ingredient">2. Most I=
nfluential Individual Ingredient<a target=3D"_self" href=3D"http://localhos=
t:8888/notebooks/Downloads/MLCapstone.ipynb#2.-Most-Influential-Individual-=
Ingredient" class=3D"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</a></h3=
>
<p> Cement showed the strongest positive individual correlation with compre=
ssive strength among the ingredients, at approximately <strong>0.488</stron=
g>. This suggests that higher cement content is generally associated with h=
igher concrete strength, although strength depends on the combined mix comp=
osition.</p>
<h3 data-jupyter-id=3D"3.-Effect-of-Curing-Age">3. Effect of Curing Age<a t=
arget=3D"_self" href=3D"http://localhost:8888/notebooks/Downloads/MLCapston=
e.ipynb#3.-Effect-of-Curing-Age" class=3D"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noo=
pener">=C2=B6</a></h3>
<p> Concrete strength generally increased as curing age increased. However,=
 the relationship was nonlinear: strength increased more rapidly during ear=
lier curing periods and tended to grow more gradually at later ages. This i=
ndicates that a simple straight-line relationship does not fully explain st=
rength development.</p>
<h3 data-jupyter-id=3D"4.-Importance-of-Water-to-Cement-Ratio">4. Importanc=
e of Water-to-Cement Ratio<a target=3D"_self" href=3D"http://localhost:8888=
/notebooks/Downloads/MLCapstone.ipynb#4.-Importance-of-Water-to-Cement-Rati=
o" class=3D"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</a></h3>
<p>The water-to-cement ratio had a correlation of approximately <strong>-0.=
489</strong> with compressive strength, indicating that higher ratios were =
generally associated with lower strength. Its relationship was slightly str=
onger in absolute terms than cement alone (0.488), demonstrating the import=
ance of considering ingredient proportions rather than individual quantitie=
s in isolation.</p>
<h3 data-jupyter-id=3D"5.-Model-Performance">5. Model Performance<a target=
=3D"_self" href=3D"http://localhost:8888/notebooks/Downloads/MLCapstone.ipy=
nb#5.-Model-Performance" class=3D"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noopener">=
=C2=B6</a></h3>
<p> Three regression approaches were compared using the same grouped traini=
ng and testing split. The mean baseline performed poorly, while Linear Regr=
ession achieved an R=C2=B2 of <strong>0.801</strong> and Random Forest achi=
eved an R=C2=B2 of <strong>0.911</strong>.</p>
<p> Random Forest produced the best results, with an MAE of <strong>3.49 MP=
a</strong> and RMSE of <strong>4.55 MPa</strong>, demonstrating its ability=
 to capture more complex relationships in the dataset than the linear model=
.</p>
<h3 data-jupyter-id=3D"6.-Largest-Prediction-Errors">6. Largest Prediction =
Errors<a target=3D"_self" href=3D"http://localhost:8888/notebooks/Downloads=
/MLCapstone.ipynb#6.-Largest-Prediction-Errors" class=3D"jp-InternalAnchorL=
ink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</a></h3>
<p> The model's largest errors included underprediction of some high-streng=
th concrete samples and overprediction of some lower-strength samples. Some=
 difficult predictions also involved early curing ages. This suggests that =
the model is less accurate at certain extremes and that prediction errors a=
re not evenly distributed across all concrete mixes.</p>
<h3 data-jupyter-id=3D"7.-Cost-Optimisation-and-Recommended-Mix">7. Cost Op=
timisation and Recommended Mix<a target=3D"_self" href=3D"http://localhost:=
8888/notebooks/Downloads/MLCapstone.ipynb#7.-Cost-Optimisation-and-Recommen=
ded-Mix" class=3D"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</a></h3>
<p> The optimisation analysis evaluated 374 observed candidate recipes usin=
g illustrative ingredient prices. Recipe 359 was identified as the lowest-c=
ost candidate meeting the conservative predicted-strength screening thresho=
ld of 48.9 MPa.</p>
<p> Its predicted compressive strength was <strong>52.16 MPa</strong>, with=
 an estimated ingredient cost of <strong>=C2=A3107.98 per cubic metre</stro=
ng>. This exceeds the required 40 MPa target by 12.16 MPa.</p>
<p> The recommendation is based on the available candidate recipes and assu=
med ingredient prices. Actual supplier quotations, production consideration=
s and laboratory testing are required before commercial adoption.</p>
<h3 data-jupyter-id=3D"Overall-Conclusion">Overall Conclusion<a target=3D"_=
self" href=3D"http://localhost:8888/notebooks/Downloads/MLCapstone.ipynb#Ov=
erall-Conclusion" class=3D"jp-InternalAnchorLink" rel=3D"noopener">=C2=B6</=
a></h3>
<p> The findings demonstrate that concrete compressive strength is influenc=
ed by multiple interacting factors, particularly cement content, water-to-c=
ement ratio and curing age. Random Forest provided the strongest predictive=
 performance among the models evaluated.</p>
<p>The cost optimisation exercise also demonstrated how machine learning ca=
n support more informed concrete mix selection by balancing predicted stren=
gth requirements with estimated ingredient costs. The developed Meridian Re=
ady-Mix prototype provides a foundation for data-driven concrete mix evalua=
tion and decision support.</p>
